---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.id === "frc5417")!;
---

<Base
  title="FRC Team 5417, Jamal Mammadzada"
  description="Fabrication and pit crew work for Allen Eagle Robotics, FRC Team 5417, plus competition deployment of the FRCMOB scouting platform."
  progress={true}
>
  <article>
    <header class="wrap case-head">
      <p class="mono">
        <a href="/work/">Work</a> / Case study {project.index}
      </p>
      <h1 class="h-xl case-title">FRC Team 5417</h1>
      <p class="lede narrow case-lede">
        Allen Eagle Robotics builds a new competition robot every year on a
        six-week clock. I work fabrication during build season and the pit during
        events. In February 2026 I began building FRCMOB for the team, and Team
        5417 has since used the platform at a real competition.
      </p>

      <dl class="meta-grid case-meta">
        {
          project.meta.map((m) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{m.label}</dt>
              <dd>{m.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <p class="note case-note">
        The awards below were won by the team. I was a member of it, and that's
        how they're stated.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="what">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="what">What the work is</h2>

      <p>
        FRC gives every team the same rough shape of problem: a game is announced,
        and about six weeks later you need a working robot. I came in on the
        fabrication side, making and assembling the parts that become the machine,
        through a build season where the design is still changing around you.
      </p>

      <p>
        At competition I work the pit. That's the turnaround between matches:
        battery swaps, checking the robot over, fixing whatever came loose, and
        getting it back out on the field in time for the next one. It's
        unglamorous and it's the difference between three matches and six.
      </p>

      <p>
        Being in the pit is also how I ended up writing software for the team. You
        hear every strategy conversation that happens between matches, and you
        notice how thin the information behind those decisions actually is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="areas">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="areas">Areas</h2>

      <div class="areas-grid">
        {
          project.stack.map((group) => (
            <div class="area-block reveal">
              <p class="mono area-head">{group.group}</p>
              <ul role="list">
                {group.items.map((i) => <li>{i}</li>)}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="lesson">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="lesson">Deploying FRCMOB with the team</h2>

      <p>
        I began FRCMOB in February 2026 because the team needed a better way to
        collect and use match evidence. It supports multiple scouts, offline
        submissions, and strategy analysis, and Team 5417 has used it at a real
        competition.
      </p>

      <p>
        What made it grow was watching the limits of that approach from the pit.
        Scouting data is only as good as the attention of the person filling out
        the form, and six robots move at once for two and a half minutes. The
        gaps were always in the same place: the teams nobody was assigned to
        watch were often the ones you most needed to know about by alliance
        selection.
      </p>

      <p>
        The footage, meanwhile, has all of it. That's the observation behind the
        computer-vision work in <a href="/work/frcmob/">FRCMOB</a>. I am still
        improving its auto-scouting, offline collection, mobile reliability,
        model evaluation, and strategy tools.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fabrication taught me the other half of it. A part that works on the
        bench and fails at competition is a part that failed, and nobody cares
        why. That's the same standard I hold the software to.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="team">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="team">Team results</h2>

      <ul class="award-list" role="list">
        <li>
          <span class="mono award-year">2025</span>
          <div>
            <p class="award-name">Fort Worth District Event, FIRST Impact Award</p>
            <p class="award-note">Won by the team; I was a member of it.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mono award-year">2025</span>
          <div>
            <p class="award-name">Tomball District Event, Winner</p>
            <p class="award-note">Won by the team; I was a member of it.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <nav class="wrap section case-next" aria-label="More work">
      <a href="/work/notetaker/" class="next-link">
        <span class="mono">Next case study</span>
        <span class="h-md">Notetaker →</span>
      </a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-head {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .case-head > .mono {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .case-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .case-lede {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .case-meta {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .case-note {
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .sec-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .areas-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(190px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  .area-block {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .area-head {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .area-block ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .award-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .award-list li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 4rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .award-list li:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .award-year {
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    padding-top: 0.25rem;
  }

  .award-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .award-note {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .case-next {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .next-link {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    padding: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .next-link .h-md {
    transition: color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .next-link:hover .h-md {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
